    "case_id": "480",
    "case_summary": "A 31-year-old man with no medical history of note was buried under snow during a ski accident at 2500 m above sea level. His hand remained exposed, leading to quick discovery and excavation after 3-5 minutes. The patient never lost consciousness and recalled being unable to breathe due to compacted snow blocking his airways. When uncovered, he was cyanotic but conscious and able to clear his airway himself. The patient denied hitting any rocks or objects during burial.\n\nInitial examination revealed:- Fully oriented patient- Peripheral and central cyanosis- Tachypnoea- Frank haemoptysis- No injuries on Advanced Trauma Life Support body check- No pain on thoracic compression- No oral bleeding wounds- Bilateral rales and crackles on pulmonary auscultation- Oxygen saturation: 93% on 100% oxygen via mask- Normal heart rate and blood pressure\n\nLaboratory Tests:- Routine laboratory results, including haemoglobin (initial: 168 g/L), B-type natriuretic peptide, and troponin concentrations were normal- Follow-up hemoglobin: 135 g/L on day 1Imaging Studies:- CT scan findings:  Image Title: Axial CT scans  Image Description: Extensive macular alveolar infiltrations throughout both lungs, showing both apical and basal infiltrations  Additional Details: No signs of trauma notedFollow-up Tests (4 weeks post-incident):- Pulmonary function tests: Normal- Echocardiography: Normal",
